.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command is a multi-purposes certificate handling command.
It can be used to print certificate information, convert certificates to
various forms, edit certificate trust settings, generate certificates
from scratch or from certification requests and then self-signing them
or signing them like a "micro CA".
.Pp
Generated certificates bear X.509 version 3. Unless specified otherwise,
key identifier extensions are included as described in
.Xr x509v3_config 5 .
.Pp
Since there are a large number of options they will split up into
various sections.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss Input, Output, and General Purpose Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -in Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
This specifies the input to read a certificate from or the input file
for reading a certificate request if the
.Sy -req
flag is used.
In both cases this defaults to standard input.
.Pp
This option cannot be combined with the
.Sy -new
flag.
.It Sy -passin Em arg
The key and certificate file password source.
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -new
Generate a certificate from scratch, not using an input certificate or
certificate request.
So this excludes the
.Sy -in
and
.Sy -req
options.
Instead, the
.Sy -set_subject
option needs to be given.
The public key to include can be given with the
.Sy -force_pubkey
option and defaults to the key given with the
.Sy -key
(or
.Sy -signkey )
option, which implies self-signature.
.It Sy -x509toreq
Output a PKCS#10 certificate request (rather than a certificate).
The
.Sy -key
(or
.Sy -signkey )
option must be used to provide the private key for self-signing; the
corresponding public key is placed in the subjectPKInfo field.
.Pp
X.509 extensions included in a certificate input are not copied by
default.
X.509 extensions to be added can be specified using the
.Sy -extfile
option.
.It Sy -req
By default a certificate is expected on input.
With this option a PKCS#10 certificate request is expected instead,
which must be correctly self-signed.
.Pp
X.509 extensions included in the request are not copied by default.
X.509 extensions to be added can be specified using the
.Sy -extfile
option.
.It Sy -copy_extensions Em arg
Determines how to handle X.509 extensions when converting from a
certificate to a request using the
.Sy -x509toreq
option or converting from a request to a certificate using the
.Sy -req
option.
If
.Em arg
is
.Sy none
or this option is not present then extensions are ignored.
If
.Em arg
is
.Sy copy
or
.Sy copyall
then all extensions are copied, except that subject identifier and
authority key identifier extensions are not taken over when producing a
certificate request.
.Pp
The
.Sy -ext
option can be used to further restrict which extensions to copy.
.It Sy -inform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The input file format to use; by default PEM is tried first.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -vfyopt Em nm Ns : Ns Em v
Pass options to the signature algorithm during verify operations.
Names and values of these options are algorithm-specific.
.It Sy -key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
This option provides the private key for signing a new certificate or
certificate request.
Unless
.Sy -force_pubkey
is given, the corresponding public key is placed in the new certificate
or certificate request, resulting in a self-signature.
.Pp
This option cannot be used in conjunction with the
.Sy -CA
option.
.Pp
It sets the issuer name to the subject name (i.e., makes it
self-issued).
Unless the
.Sy -preserve_dates
option is supplied, it sets the validity start date to the current time
and the end date to a value determined by the
.Sy -days
option.
Start date and end date can also be explicitly supplied with options
.Sy -not_before
and
.Sy -not_after .
.It Sy -signkey Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
This option is an alias of
.Sy -key .
.It Sy -keyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The key input format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -out Em filename
This specifies the output filename to write to or standard output by
default.
.It Sy -outform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The output format; the default is
.Sy PEM .
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -nocert
Do not output a certificate (except for printing as requested by below
options).
.It Sy -noout
This option prevents output except for printing as requested by below
options.
.El
.Ss Certificate Printing Options
Note: the
.Sy -alias
and
.Sy -purpose
options are also printing options but are described in the
.Sx Trust Settings
section.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -dateopt
Specify the date output format.
Values are: rfc_822 and iso_8601. Defaults to rfc_822.
.It Sy -text
Prints out the certificate in text form.
Full details are printed including the public key, signature algorithms,
issuer and subject names, serial number any extensions present and any
trust settings.
.It Sy -certopt Em option
Customise the print format used with
.Sy -text .
The
.Em option
argument can be a single option or multiple options separated by commas.
The
.Sy -certopt
switch may be also be used more than once to set multiple options.
See the
.Sx Text Printing Flags
section for more information.
.It Sy -fingerprint
Calculates and prints the digest of the DER encoded version of the
entire certificate (see digest options).
This is commonly called a "fingerprint".
Because of the nature of message digests, the fingerprint of a
certificate is unique to that certificate and two certificates with the
same fingerprint can be considered to be the same.
.It Sy -alias
Prints the certificate "alias" (nickname), if any.
.It Sy -serial
Prints the certificate serial number.
.It Sy -startdate
Prints out the start date of the certificate, that is the notBefore
date.
.It Sy -enddate
Prints out the expiry date of the certificate, that is the notAfter
date.
.It Sy -dates
Prints out the start and expiry dates of a certificate.
.It Sy -subject
Prints the subject name.
.It Sy -issuer
Prints the issuer name.
.It Sy -nameopt Em option
This specifies how the subject or issuer names are displayed.
See
.Xr openssl-namedisplay-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -email
Prints the email address(es) if any.
.It Sy -hash
Synonym for "-subject_hash" for backward compatibility reasons.
.It Sy -subject_hash
Prints the "hash" of the certificate subject name.
This is used in OpenSSL to form an index to allow certificates in a
directory to be looked up by subject name.
.It Sy -subject_hash_old
Prints the "hash" of the certificate subject name using the older
algorithm as used by OpenSSL before version 1.0.0.
.It Sy -issuer_hash
Prints the "hash" of the certificate issuer name.
.It Sy -issuer_hash_old
Prints the "hash" of the certificate issuer name using the older
algorithm as used by OpenSSL before version 1.0.0.
.It Sy -ext Em extensions
Prints out the certificate extensions in text form.
Can also be used to restrict which extensions to copy.
Extensions are specified with a comma separated string, e.g.,
"subjectAltName, subjectKeyIdentifier".
See the
.Xr x509v3_config 5
manual page for the extension names.
.It Sy -ocspid
Prints the OCSP hash values for the subject name and public key.
.It Sy -ocsp_uri
Prints the OCSP responder address(es) if any.
.It Sy -purpose
This option performs tests on the certificate extensions and outputs the
results.
For a more complete description see
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1 .
.It Sy -pubkey
Prints the certificate's SubjectPublicKeyInfo block in PEM format.
.It Sy -modulus
This option prints out the value of the modulus of the public key
contained in the certificate.
.El
.Ss Certificate Checking Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -checkend Em arg
Checks if the certificate expires within the next
.Em arg
seconds and exits nonzero if yes it will expire or zero if not.
.It Sy -checkhost Em host
Check that the certificate matches the specified host.
.It Sy -checkemail Em email
Check that the certificate matches the specified email address.
.It Sy -checkip Em ipaddr
Check that the certificate matches the specified IP address.
.El
.Ss Certificate Output Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -set_serial Em n
Specifies the serial number to use.
This option can be used with the
.Sy -key ,
.Sy -signkey ,
or
.Sy -CA
options.
If used in conjunction with the
.Sy -CA
option the serial number file (as specified by the
.Sy -CAserial
option) is not used.
.Pp
The serial number can be decimal or hex (if preceded by
.Qo Li 0x Qc Ns ).
.It Sy -next_serial
Set the serial to be one more than the number in the certificate.
.It Sy -not_before Em date
This allows the start date to be explicitly set.
The format of the date is YYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 UTCTime
structure), or YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 GeneralizedTime
structure).
In both formats, seconds SS and timezone Z must be present.
Alternatively, you can also use "today".
.Pp
Cannot be used together with the
.Sy -preserve_dates
option.
.It Sy -not_after Em date
This allows the expiry date to be explicitly set.
The format of the date is YYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 UTCTime
structure), or YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 GeneralizedTime
structure).
In both formats, seconds SS and timezone Z must be present.
Alternatively, you can also use "today".
.Pp
Cannot be used together with the
.Sy -preserve_dates
option.
This overrides the option
.Sy -days .
.It Sy -days Em arg
Specifies the number of days from today until a newly generated
certificate expires.
The default is 30.
.Pp
Cannot be used together with the option
.Sy -preserve_dates .
If option
.Sy -not_after
is set, the explicit expiry date takes precedence.
.It Sy -preserve_dates
When signing a certificate, preserve "notBefore" and "notAfter" dates of
any input certificate instead of adjusting them to current time and
duration.
Cannot be used together with the options
.Sy -days ,
.Sy -not_before
and
.Sy -not_after .
.It Sy -set_issuer Em arg
When a certificate is created set its issuer name to the given value.
.Pp
See
.Sy -set_subject
on how the arg must be formatted.
.It Sy -set_subject Em arg
When a certificate is created set its subject name to the given value.
When the certificate is self-signed the issuer name is set to the same
value, unless the
.Sy -set_issuer
option is given.
.Pp
The arg must be formatted as
.Qo Li /type0=value0/type1=value1/type2=... Qc .
Special characters may be escaped by
.Qo Li \e Qc
(backslash), whitespace is retained.
Empty values are permitted, but the corresponding type will not be
included in the certificate.
Giving a single
.Qo Li / Qc
will lead to an empty sequence of RDNs (a NULL-DN). Multi-valued RDNs
can be formed by placing a
.Qo Li + Qc
character instead of a
.Qo Li / Qc
between the AttributeValueAssertions (AVAs) that specify the members of
the set.
Example:
.Pp
.Qo Li /DC=org/DC=OpenSSL/DC=users/UID=123456+CN=John Doe Qc
.Pp
This option can be used with the
.Sy -new
and
.Sy -force_pubkey
options to create a new certificate without providing an input
certificate or certificate request.
.It Sy -subj Em arg
This option is an alias of
.Sy -set_subject .
.It Sy -force_pubkey Em filename
When a new certificate or certificate request is created set its public
key to the given key instead of the key contained in the input or given
with the
.Sy -key
(or
.Sy -signkey )
option.
If the input contains no public key but a private key, its public part
is used.
.Pp
This option can be used in conjunction with b<-new> and
.Sy -set_subject
to directly generate a certificate containing any desired public key.
.Pp
This option is also useful for creating self-issued certificates that
are not self-signed, for instance when the key cannot be used for
signing, such as DH.
.It Sy -clrext
When transforming a certificate to a new certificate by default all
certificate extensions are retained.
.Pp
When transforming a certificate or certificate request, the
.Sy -clrext
option prevents taking over any extensions from the source.
In any case, when producing a certificate request, neither subject
identifier nor authority key identifier extensions are included.
.It Sy -extfile Em filename
Configuration file containing certificate and request X.509 extensions
to add.
.It Sy -extensions Em section
The section in the extfile to add X.509 extensions from.
If this option is not specified then the extensions should either be
contained in the unnamed (default) section or the default section should
contain a variable called "extensions" which contains the section to
use.
.Pp
See the
.Xr x509v3_config 5
manual page for details of the extension section format.
.Pp
Unless specified otherwise, key identifier extensions are included as
described in
.Xr x509v3_config 5 .
.It Sy -sigopt Em nm Ns : Ns Em v
Pass options to the signature algorithm during sign operations.
This option may be given multiple times.
Names and values provided using this option are algorithm-specific.
.It Sy -badsig
Corrupt the signature before writing it; this can be useful for testing.
.It Sy - Ns Em digest
The digest to use.
This affects any signing or printing option that uses a message digest,
such as the
.Sy -fingerprint ,
.Sy -key ,
and
.Sy -CA
options.
Any digest supported by the
.Xr openssl-dgst 1
command can be used.
If not specified then SHA1 is used with
.Sy -fingerprint
or the default digest for the signing algorithm is used, typically
SHA256.
.El
.Ss Micro-CA Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -CA Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Specifies the "CA" certificate to be used for signing.
When present, this behaves like a "micro CA" as follows: The subject
name of the "CA" certificate is placed as issuer name in the new
certificate, which is then signed using the "CA" key given as detailed
below.
.Pp
This option cannot be used in conjunction with
.Sy -key
(or
.Sy -signkey Ns ).
This option is normally combined with the
.Sy -req
option referencing a CSR. Without the
.Sy -req
option the input must be an existing certificate unless the
.Sy -new
option is given, which generates a certificate from scratch.
.It Sy -CAform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 ,
The format for the CA certificate; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -CAkey Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Sets the CA private key to sign a certificate with.
The private key must match the public key of the certificate given with
.Sy -CA .
If this option is not provided then the key must be present in the
.Sy -CA
input.
.It Sy -CAkeyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The format for the CA key; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -CAserial Em filename
Sets the CA serial number file to use.
.Pp
When creating a certificate with this option and with the
.Sy -CA
option, the certificate serial number is stored in the given file.
This file consists of one line containing an even number of hex digits
with the serial number used last time.
After reading this number, it is incremented and used, and the file is
updated.
.Pp
The default filename consists of the CA certificate file base name with
.Pa .srl
appended.
For example if the CA certificate file is called
.Pa mycacert.pem
it expects to find a serial number file called
.Pa mycacert.srl .
.Pp
If the
.Sy -CA
option is specified and neither <-CAserial> or <-CAcreateserial> is
given and the default serial number file does not exist, a random number
is generated; this is the recommended practice.
.It Sy -CAcreateserial
With this option and the
.Sy -CA
option the CA serial number file is created if it does not exist.
A random number is generated, used for the certificate, and saved into
the serial number file determined as described above.
.El
.Ss Trust Settings
A
.Sy trusted certificate
is an ordinary certificate which has several additional pieces of
information attached to it such as the permitted and prohibited uses of
the certificate and possibly an "alias" (nickname).
.Pp
Normally when a certificate is being verified at least one certificate
must be "trusted".
By default a trusted certificate must be stored locally and must be a
root CA: any certificate chain ending in this CA is then usable for any
purpose.
.Pp
Trust settings currently are only used with a root CA. They allow a
finer control over the purposes the root CA can be used for.
For example, a CA may be trusted for SSL client but not SSL server use.
.Pp
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for more information on the meaning of trust settings.
.Pp
Future versions of OpenSSL will recognize trust settings on any
certificate: not just root CAs.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -trustout
Mark any certificate PEM output as <trusted> certificate rather than
ordinary.
An ordinary or trusted certificate can be input but by default an
ordinary certificate is output and any trust settings are discarded.
With the
.Sy -trustout
option a trusted certificate is output.
A trusted certificate is automatically output if any trust settings are
modified.
.It Sy -setalias Em arg
Sets the "alias" of the certificate.
This will allow the certificate to be referred to using a nickname for
example "Steve's Certificate".
.It Sy -clrtrust
Clears all the permitted or trusted uses of the certificate.
.It Sy -addtrust Em arg
Adds a trusted certificate use.
Any object name can be used here but currently only
.Sy clientAuth ,
.Sy serverAuth ,
.Sy emailProtection ,
and
.Sy anyExtendedKeyUsage
are defined.
As of OpenSSL 1.1.0, the last of these blocks all purposes when rejected
or enables all purposes when trusted.
Other OpenSSL applications may define additional uses.
.It Sy -clrreject
Clears all the prohibited or rejected uses of the certificate.
.It Sy -addreject Em arg
Adds a prohibited trust anchor purpose.
It accepts the same values as the
.Sy -addtrust
option.
.El
.Ss Generic options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Ss Text Printing Flags
As well as customising the name printing format, it is also possible to
customise the actual fields printed using the
.Sy certopt
option when the
.Sy text
option is present.
The default behaviour is to print all fields.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy compatible
Use the old format.
This is equivalent to specifying no printing options at all.
.It Sy no_header
Don't print header information: that is the lines saying "Certificate"
and "Data".
.It Sy no_version
Don't print out the version number.
.It Sy no_serial
Don't print out the serial number.
.It Sy no_signame
Don't print out the signature algorithm used.
.It Sy no_validity
Don't print the validity, that is the
.Sy notBefore
and
.Sy notAfter
fields.
.It Sy no_subject
Don't print out the subject name.
.It Sy no_issuer
Don't print out the issuer name.
.It Sy no_pubkey
Don't print out the public key.
.It Sy no_sigdump
Don't give a hexadecimal dump of the certificate signature.
.It Sy no_aux
Don't print out certificate trust information.
.It Sy no_extensions
Don't print out any X509V3 extensions.
.It Sy ext_default
Retain default extension behaviour: attempt to print out unsupported
certificate extensions.
.It Sy ext_error
Print an error message for unsupported certificate extensions.
.It Sy ext_parse
ASN1 parse unsupported extensions.
.It Sy ext_dump
Hex dump unsupported extensions.
.It Sy ca_default
The value used by
.Xr openssl-ca 1 ,
equivalent to
.Sy no_issuer ,
.Sy no_pubkey ,
.Sy no_header ,
and
.Sy no_version .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Note: in these examples the '\e' means the example should be all on one
line.
.Pp
Print the contents of a certificate:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -text
.Ed
.Pp
Print the "Subject Alternative Name" extension of a certificate:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -ext subjectAltName
.Ed
.Pp
Print more extensions of a certificate:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -ext subjectAltName,nsCertType
.Ed
.Pp
Print the certificate serial number:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -serial
.Ed
.Pp
Print the certificate subject name:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -subject
.Ed
.Pp
Print the certificate subject name in RFC2253 form:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -subject -nameopt RFC2253
.Ed
.Pp
Print the certificate subject name in oneline form on a terminal
supporting UTF8:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -subject -nameopt oneline,-esc_msb
.Ed
.Pp
Print the certificate SHA1 fingerprint:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -sha1 -in cert.pem -noout -fingerprint
.Ed
.Pp
Convert a certificate from PEM to DER format:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -inform PEM -out cert.der -outform DER
.Ed
.Pp
Convert a certificate to a certificate request:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -x509toreq -in cert.pem -out req.pem -key key.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Convert a certificate request into a self-signed certificate using
extensions for a CA:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -req -in careq.pem -extfile openssl.cnf -extensions v3_ca \e
        -key key.pem -out cacert.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Sign a certificate request using the CA certificate above and add user
certificate extensions:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -req -in req.pem -extfile openssl.cnf -extensions v3_usr \e
        -CA cacert.pem -CAkey key.pem -CAcreateserial
.Ed
.Pp
Set a certificate to be trusted for SSL client use and change set its
alias to "Steve's Class 1 CA"
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -addtrust clientAuth \e
        -setalias "Steve's Class 1 CA" -out trust.pem
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
The conversion to UTF8 format used with the name options assumes that
T61Strings use the ISO8859-1 character set.
This is wrong but Netscape and MSIE do this as do many certificates.
So although this is incorrect it is more likely to print the majority of
certificates correctly.
.Pp
The
.Sy -email
option searches the subject name and the subject alternative name
extension.
Only unique email addresses will be printed out: it will not print the
same address more than once.
.Sh BUGS
It is possible to produce invalid certificates or requests by specifying
the wrong private key, using unsuitable X.509 extensions, or using
inconsistent options in some cases: these should be checked.
.Pp
There should be options to explicitly set such things as start and end
dates rather than an offset from the current time.
